text('station','The last service','short fiction','Original fictional extract; a teenager waits at a regional station.', '''The station clock had stopped at six, but the buses kept arriving late. Mara held her acceptance letter under the timetable, sheltering its blue ink from the rain. Her father stood beside the locked ticket window. He had brought a bag of oranges, as though a journey could be provisioned for one afternoon.
“There will be shops,” she said.
“There will,” he answered, and tightened the knot in the bag.
Beyond the fence, the highway lamps came on one by one. Mara folded the letter until the university crest disappeared. When the bus finally turned the corner, neither of them moved. The driver opened the door. Her father put the oranges on the step, leaving both hands free.''')
text('return','A borrowed key','short fiction','Original fictional extract; an adult returns to a childhood home.', '''The new owners had painted the gate yellow. I remembered it as green, although my sister said it had always been blue. We stood outside with a key that no longer fitted anything.
Inside, a child was practising scales. The wrong note came at the same place each time. My sister laughed; I wanted to tell the child how to move a thumb beneath the hand. Instead I put the key in my pocket.
“We should knock,” she said.
I said we should let them finish. Neither of us was talking about the music. On the walk back, she described a birthday I could not remember. I agreed with every detail. The key made a small, persistent shape against my leg.''')
text('garden','A garden after hours','community opinion','Fictional newsletter opinion addressing neighbourhood residents.', '''At five o’clock our street becomes a row of closed doors. Behind each door someone buys herbs in plastic, while the vacant corner grows weeds for free. A shared garden would not solve loneliness. It might give loneliness somewhere to sit.
Some neighbours worry that a garden will demand time they do not have. That is a fair concern. Start with two beds, a water roster and one open afternoon a month. Participation should be an invitation, not a test of citizenship.
We already share the footpath, the stormwater and the summer heat. We can share a little shade. Come to the corner on Saturday, bring a question, and help decide what belongs there.''')
text('council','Corner-site update','administrative notice','Fictional council notice for residents; all figures are invented task data.', '''The corner-site proposal is under review. Two raised planting beds and a lockable storage unit are included in the preliminary design. Access arrangements, water use and maintenance responsibility remain unresolved.
Residents may submit comments by 5 pm on Friday. A public meeting will be held next Tuesday at the community hall. No construction date has been confirmed. The estimated initial cost is $4,800, excluding volunteer labour and ongoing water charges.
Written submissions will be summarised for the committee. Contact details will be removed from the public summary. Residents who need a paper form can collect one from the library. Attendance at the meeting is not required to make a submission.''')
text('library','Keep the lights on','persuasive speech','Fictional speech by a student at a council meeting.', '''You call the library an optional service. For the student whose home has no quiet desk, it is a classroom. For the new resident reading a form in an unfamiliar language, it is a doorway. For the night-shift worker collecting a reserved book, it is a small promise that public life has room for them.
I understand the budget is limited. I am not asking you to pretend that costs disappear. I am asking you to publish the alternatives before you shorten our opening hours.
Count the loans, certainly. Count the people who arrive with nothing to borrow and everything to learn. A closed door is cheaper to maintain. It is also a very poor welcome.''')
text('budget','Opening-hours review','manager statement','Fictional service manager explaining proposed library changes.', '''Our proposal concentrates staffing on the periods of highest recorded demand. Longer Saturday sessions would replace two late weekday sessions. This is a proposal, not a final decision.
Loan transactions provide one measure of use; they do not capture every reason a person enters a library. We will therefore collect room bookings, visitor counts and written accounts of after-hours needs before the committee votes.
Keeping the present timetable would require either additional funding or a reduction in another service. Neither choice should be hidden. We welcome evidence about who benefits from each session and who would lose access if it ended. A decision supported by a single number would be simpler. It would not necessarily be better.''')
text('bridge','The repaired bridge','news report','Fictional local report; quotations belong to invented speakers.', '''The river footbridge reopened on Monday after six weeks of repairs. Council engineer Leah Tan said the new deck met the inspection requirements. “The structure is ready for use,” she said.
At the opening, mayor Paul Reed called the bridge “a connection restored”. The report released by the council lists the final cost but does not identify the subcontractors.
Shop owner Amal Yusuf said the closure had added twenty minutes to her walk to work. “It is good to have it back,” she said. A wheelchair user at the event asked why the approach path remained uneven. The mayor said the path would be considered in a separate project. By noon the ribbon had gone, and pedestrians were crossing between the two temporary warning signs.''')
text('progress','A faster tomorrow','promotional announcement','Fictional development brochure addressed to prospective investors.', '''A faster tomorrow begins here. The new riverside precinct brings premium spaces, smart access and a connected lifestyle to an underused site. Walk from work to coffee in minutes. Make every moment count.
Our artist’s impression presents a bright, open destination. Final finishes and landscaping are subject to approval. Register your interest for priority updates.
The precinct will replace several small tenancies with an integrated development. Relocation arrangements will be discussed with current occupants. We believe change creates opportunity. Discover what is possible when a place is designed around ambition.''')
text('work','The names on the wall','personal essay','Original fictional reflective essay by a former repair-shop apprentice.', '''When the shop closed, I took a photograph of the wall rather than the sign. The wall held outlines where spanners had hung, each one drawn by someone who hated wasting time. It also held names, pencil marks and a calendar that had not been turned since March.
The redevelopment brochure called the site underused. It was an accurate word if use meant floor area sold. It was an odd word for a room where I had learned to ask for help without apologising.
I do not want every old building preserved. The roof leaked and the stairs were unsafe. I want a language for change that can admit a loss without treating that admission as opposition. My photograph does not repair the roof. It keeps the names legible.''')
text('phone','The notification','short fiction','Original fictional extract about digital attention.', '''The screen lit up while Niko was speaking. I turned it face down, a small ceremony of virtue. It vibrated against the table. Niko stopped and looked at my hand.
“You can answer it,” he said.
“I’m listening.”
Outside the café, a delivery rider waited through a second red light. I watched the rider, then the dark rectangle by my cup. Niko began again, more slowly this time. He was describing the hospital corridor. I nodded at a word I had not heard.
When the phone finally fell silent, the conversation had become something I would need to ask permission to re-enter. Niko stirred a coffee that had no sugar in it.''')
text('review','A small stage, a large silence','performance review','Fictional critic responding to an invented stage production.', '''The production places two chairs on a bare stage and asks them to carry a town. For most of the evening, they do. When the departing daughter leaves her chair facing the audience, the empty seat becomes more persuasive than the father’s final speech.
The performance occasionally mistakes stillness for depth. A long pause after every accusation flattens the differences between anger, embarrassment and grief. Yet the restrained lighting allows the actors’ hands to register changes that a crowded set might conceal.
This is an uneven production with a precise central insight: absence is not empty. The last image earns the silence that follows it. Some earlier scenes merely wait for that image to arrive.''')
text('memoir','Learning the river','memoir-style prose','Original invented memoir-style text; not a real person’s testimony.', '''My grandfather taught me the river by its bends, not by the map. I learned the names on the map at school and returned home certain I could correct him. He listened, then asked which name would tell me where the water slowed after rain.
I had no answer. He did not laugh.
Years later, I still need maps. They tell me distances he never needed to travel. His descriptions tell me where to look when a distance is not enough. I used to imagine learning as a staircase: each new step made the old one unnecessary. Now I think of the riverbank, where several paths can reach the same water and each path shows you something the others conceal.''')
text('poem','Inventory at the gate','poem','Original teaching poem. Use for studied-text skills; not a Section One stimulus.', '''One key,
three oranges,
a letter folded small.
The gate keeps no inventory
of what we mean to carry.
I leave my certainty beside the hinge.
It does not fit the bag.''')
text('drama','At the committee table','drama extract','Original fictional scene. Use for studied-text skills; not a Section One stimulus.', '''CHAIR: We have heard from everyone.
RESIDENT: Everyone who could come at two in the afternoon.
[The chair moves the microphone closer to himself.]
CHAIR: The minutes will record your concern.
RESIDENT: Will they record the empty chairs?
[A long pause. A cleaner appears at the open door, then steps back.]
CHAIR: Next item.''')
text('film','The corridor: a shot sequence','film storyboard','Original invented screen sequence; no real film is represented.', '''SHOT 1: A long shot shows a worker entering an empty hospital corridor. Fluorescent light washes the walls blue. Footsteps echo; there is no music.
SHOT 2: A close-up shows the worker’s hand tightening around a folded paper. The words on it are not visible. The camera stays at eye level.
SHOT 3: A reverse shot shows a closed door at the far end. The footsteps stop before the cut.
SHOT 4: From inside the room, the camera frames the doorway. The worker remains outside, small within the frame. A voice says, “Come in.” The cut to black occurs before the worker moves.''')
text('digital','A thread about the corner','digital conversation','Original fictional public thread; metrics are invented.', '''NEIGHBOUR A: Two garden beds will not change the world. They might change this corner. [42 likes]
NEIGHBOUR B: Who is “we”? I work Saturdays. Does my opinion still count? [18 likes]
NEIGHBOUR A: Yes. The planning form is open all week. I should have included that link.
PINNED UPDATE: Meeting date, access arrangements and paper submission details are now attached.
ADVERTISEMENT BELOW THREAD: Grow your perfect life. Buy the premium starter kit.
DISPLAY NOTE: The platform sorts replies by engagement rather than time unless the reader changes the setting.''')
